\p{Weeknotes, Spring Break edition! (For non-US readers: Spring Break is (typically) one week off from classes, approximately in the middle of the spring semester.)  This has been a relaxing and productive week with no classes to teach.}

\subtree{
  \title{Immutable arrays in Swarm}

  \subtree{
    \title{Challenge scenarios requring arrays}
  \p{Continuing my work on [immutable arrays in Swarm](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/98), this week I was playing around with creating some challenge scenarios that require the use of arrays. This is actually somewhat tricky, since you can always simulate arrays with functions—it's just less efficient.  So the general idea is to construct scenarios with a time limit, where you need the efficiency of an array to be able to get a solution that is fast enough. But even this is tricky!}

  \p{My first attempt just involved a row of randomly generated capital letters that you are supposed to sort alphabetically.  I thought you could read them all into an array and pick them up at the same time, sort the array, then lay them out in sorted order.  However, I realized that you can just pick them all up, then iterate through the capital letters one at a time in order, and place any that you happen to have of each.}

  \p{I constructed another scenario that I thought would work: there is a "key" row of randomly permuted capital letters, and another row of randomly generated capital letters.  You must sort the second row, but in the order specified by the permutation in the first row.  However, I realized there's a clever way to accomplish this in #{O(n)} time without using an array: (1) pick up all the items to be sorted; (2) scan through the key permutation, going one recursive call deeper with each item, so the recursion stack ends up storing the entire sequence; (3) unwind the recursion stack while walking along the destination row in reverse; for each item of the permutation, place all the copies of it you have in your inventory.}

  \p{Having written this, I think I should go back to my original idea, but use randomly generated strings instead of individual capital letters.  That way, the range of possible values is huge, so there's no way to simply iterate through them in order and place any that you happen to have.  (Currently, there's no way to iterate through items in your inventory...)}
  }

  \subtree{
    \title{Minimal API for immutable arrays}
  \p{It's also been interesting to think about a good minimal API for immutable arrays.  Elimination forms are easy: we just need \code{index : Array a -> Int -> a} and \code{size : Array a -> Int}.  But what about introduction forms? At this point I've implemented \code{unfold : b -> (b -> Unit + (a * b)) -> Array a} (as well as an effectful variant), but I'm starting to think that's not good enough: it assumes that you can generate the array elements in order, all at once.  Instead, I think we really want something like:
\pre\verb<<<|
accum : a -> (a -> a -> a) -> b -> (b -> Unit + (Int * a * b)) -> Array a<<<
  which unfolds (index, value) pairs starting from a seed value, starting with the default value and using the combining function for multiple values at the same index.  In Haskell you would write this as \code{Monoid a => [(Int, a)] -> Array a} but Swarm has no type classes and we don't want arrays to depend on lists, so we encode the monoid constraint via the first two arguments, and encode the list via an unfold.
  }

    \p{Is there a simpler way to do this?}
    }
}

