\subtree{
  \title{Kattis}
  \p{I had fun this week solving a few [Kattis](https://open.kattis.com/) problems in Haskell.  One particularly interesting problem was [Metro Lines](https://open.kattis.com/problems/metrolines): there is a line of stops numbered #{0} to #{N}, and we are given a bunch (up to #{2 \times 10^5}) of subway lines, each of which has a cost #{c} and visits all the stops between #{l} and #{r} inclusive.  You can get on and off of any line anywhere you want; it costs #{c \cdot |x-y|} to ride from #{x} to #{y}, where #{c} is the cost of the line.  We're supposed to print out the cheapest fare to get from station #{0} to each station from #{1} through #{N}.}

  \p{Try solving it yourself, or click to expand my solution sketch below!}

  \put\transclude/expanded{false}
  \subtree{
    \title{Solution sketch for [Metro Lines](https://open.kattis.com/problems/metrolines) (spoiler)}
    \author{brentyorgey}
    \date{2026-03-28T00:37:04Z}

    \p{The solution is to sort all the line endpoints, then scan through from left to right, keeping track of a set of active lines, sorted by cost (I just used a \code{Set} of #{(\mathit{cost}, \mathit{line ID})} pairs).  For each segment #{(k,k+1)}, we then know the cost #{c} of the cheapest line which operates on that segment. The key insight is that since we can get on and off whenever we want, we can independently choose the cheapest line for each one-unit segment; since the cost is linear in the length of the ride, we can just independently add up the cost #{c} of each one-unit segment to get the total cost.}
  }
}
